{"v":"3.0.0","project":{"accession":"PRJNA1492049","title":"Effects of dry-weather substrate supplementation frequency on performance and microbial adaptation of intermittently operated moving bed bioreactors for stormwater runoff treatment","description":"The intermittent nature of stormwater runoff, punctuated by prolonged dry-weather intervals and abrupt wet-weather events, poses a critical challenge for biological treatment systems like moving bed biofilm reactors (MBBRs), where microbes are subjected to repeated starvation-reactivation stress. In this study, effects of different dry-weather maintenance strategies were evaluated in intermittently operated MBBRs over 88 days with four wastewater supplementation frequencies, including no supplementation (MBBRC), supplementation only before rainfall (MBBR10), supplementation every five days (MBBR5), and daily supplementation (MBBR1). Chemical oxygen demand and ammonium removal in the MBBRC decreased from 80.5% and 80.9% to 56.5% and 55.9% after 55-day intermittent operation, respectively, while MBBR5 and MBBR1 maintained more than 90% of the initial treatment performance. The MBBR5 retained 77.9% of the initial attached biomass, and preserved extracellular polymeric substance protein content, cell viability and biofilm electrochemical stability. Microbial community and metagenomic analyses further showed that starvation selected for oligotrophic taxa and energy-conserving metabolic pathways, whereas periodic supplementation in MBBR5 enriched nitrifying and biofilm-associated populations and stabilized nitrogen transformation potential. Although MBBR1 achieved comparable pollutant removal, the five-day supplementation strategy of MBBR5 supported a more balanced microbial community and functional profile with substantially lower operational demand. These findings show that dry-weather substrate supplementation is not merely a maintenance measure but a key control strategy for sustaining microbial and functional resilience in stormwater MBBRs, and that five-day supplementation offers a practical, low-maintenance approach for decentralized stormwater treatment under irregular rainfall conditions.","organism":null,"data_type":"Raw sequence reads","center_name":"East China Normal University","submission_date":"2026/07/08 00:00","last_update":null},"sample_cols":["accession","title","collection_date","country","region","city","lat","lon","urban_status","urban_context","eligibility_reason","isolation_source","env_broad_scale","env_local_scale","env_medium","host","location_resolution","country_iso2","place_id","location_status","location_method","location_note","geo_loc_raw","collection_year"],"samples":[["SAMN61473525","Metagenome or environmental sample from sludge metagenome","2025-09-02","China","Shanghai","Shanghai",31.02,121.45,"probable_urban","municipal_wastewater","city-level location without sample-site confirmation",null,null,null,null,null,"coordinate","CN",1796236,"verified","text+coordinates",null,"China: Shanghai",2025],["SAMN61473526","Metagenome or environmental sample from sludge metagenome","2025-09-02","China","Shanghai","Shanghai",31.02,121.45,"probable_urban","municipal_wastewater","city-level location without sample-site confirmation",null,null,null,null,null,"coordinate","CN",1796236,"verified","text+coordinates",null,"China: Shanghai",2025],["SAMN61473527","Metagenome or environmental sample from sludge metagenome","2025-09-02","China","Shanghai","Shanghai",31.02,121.45,"probable_urban","municipal_wastewater","city-level location without sample-site confirmation",null,null,null,null,null,"coordinate","CN",1796236,"verified","text+coordinates",null,"China: Shanghai",2025],["SAMN61473528","Metagenome or environmental sample from sludge metagenome","2025-10-15","China","Shanghai","Shanghai",31.02,121.45,"probable_urban","municipal_wastewater","city-level location without sample-site confirmation",null,null,null,null,null,"coordinate","CN",1796236,"verified","tex